Each section here covers one CapTP mechanism, roughly in the order a session
meets them: references, the locator that gets you your first one, what calls
look like in flight, and the bookkeeping that keeps long-lived sessions
healthy. If capabilities themselves are still new, read
[Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) first; this page assumes you are sold and want
to know how the machinery works. If you are unsure what separates `Session`,
`Host`, and `Peer`, that is [modules]($link.page('modules')).

## A capability is a reference

The whole protocol rests on the ordinary object reference. Code that holds a
reference to an object can call it. Code that was never handed one cannot,
and there is no way to manufacture one from bytes.
[CapTP](http://erights.org/elib/distrib/captp/) is the protocol that keeps
this true between two programs on a network,
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) is its standardization, and
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) is the implementation
that ships it today.

Most things you would normally build permission infrastructure for turn out
to be ways of passing references around. Hand one along and you have
delegated. Wrap one in an object that only forwards some methods, hand out
the wrapper, and you have attenuated. Cut the wrapper and you have revoked.
[Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) walks all three with pictures.

[A capability is a reference, not a copy. Pass it as an argument and the partner can call it; without it there is nothing else to reach for.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

A call itself is a two-beat exchange. Your deliver goes out and you are
immediately holding a promise for the answer; the partner's resolve, whenever
it lands, settles that promise into a result.

[A single call, start to finish: you deliver it and immediately hold a promise, and the partner's resolve settles that promise to a result.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-call.svg'))

## Getting the first reference: locators and sturdyrefs

References only come from other references, which makes the very first one a
special problem. That is the locator's job. A locator is an address string:

```
ocapn://<designator>.<transport>
```

The **designator** names the peer: a `host:port` for `tcp`, a public key for
the keyed transports. Optional `?key=value` **hints** carry routing help,
like the socket address for a keyed listener. Hints can only help you reach
a peer; they carry no authority of their own.

Dialing a locator opens a session, and a fresh session contains exactly one
usable object: the **bootstrap object**, its front door. You fetch a
published name from it and get back a live reference. From there you are in
the reference-passing world and the locator has done its whole job.

A **sturdyref** is a locator with a **swiss number** on the end, and it names
one object rather than a peer. It is the form you can write down in a config
file. It survives the host restarting, provided the host re-registers that
name under the same identity, and redeeming it is the same dial-and-fetch,
aimed at one specific object.

| Word | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| locator | address of a peer (and how to dial it) |
| swiss number | secret name of one object at that peer |
| live reference | session authority you can call *now* |

[Everything left of the hints is an address, and none of it is authority. Only the swiss number names something you can be given.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-locator.svg'))

> **Swiss number**: holding the string *is* the permission; there is no
> permission list and no login. The name comes from the numbered Swiss bank
> account, where the number alone is the access.

The
[sturdyref diagrams](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/07-sturdyrefs-and-locators.md)
go deeper, and [Transports]($link.page('transports')) covers the netlayers a
locator can name.

## Promise pipelining

A remote call hands back a **promise** before any bytes return, and you can
call methods on that promise right away. Those calls travel to the peer and
queue against the pending answer, so they run the moment it settles, over
there, without another round trip from you.

The practical effect: you ask a task board for a column, then ask that column
for a card, and both questions cross the network in one flight. Chapter 06,
[pipeline and attenuate](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/06_pipeline_and_attenuate.zig),
runs exactly that, and the
[promises and pipelining](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/03-promises-and-pipelining.md)
diagrams draw the flow the code is checked against.

## Three-party handoff

Alice holds a reference to Carol's object and wants Bob to have it. Routing
Bob through Alice forever would leave Alice proxying a relationship she has
no part in, so CapTP gives her a way to make an introduction Bob can present
to Carol himself. After that the two of them talk directly.

The handoff runs in four stages:

1. Alice **deposits** a gift with Carol and builds a signed **give**.
2. Alice's answer to Bob carries that give (the introduction).
3. Bob **redeems** with Carol and gets a live reference.
4. Alice is off the path.

In pancake's application surface you usually never see the gift bytes. With a
Host (Zig `Bridge`, or a binding on `libpancake_driver`), a call that answers
with an introduction settles as the introduced capability inside the wait.
In the rooms chat this is the _Message_ button: your code asked the room for
a member, and what came back was the member.

Chapter 02,
[introduce and redeem](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/02_introduce_and_redeem.zig),
runs the whole exchange, and the
[handoffs](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/06-handoffs.md)
diagrams draw it.

## Which dialect answers

Two peers have to agree on a CapTP **version** and a **codec** before
anything else can happen. Each side carries a ranked menu of what it can
speak, both sides run the same intersection rule, and the most-preferred
entry both offer wins. None of this travels on the wire except the final
version string inside `op:start-session`. If the menus share nothing, no
session opens.

Worth keeping straight: Syrup and Cap'n Proto are codecs, two ways of writing
the same message down. Session logic never changes with the codec, and
[Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) weighs the two that ship.

[Both peers run the same rule over the two menus and reach the same pick.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-negotiation.svg'))

[The dialect depends on who answers: the canonical floor against Goblins, pancake's own profile between two pancake peers.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-dialects.svg'))

Against a Goblins peer the menu lands on CapTP 1.0 over Syrup, the interop
floor, and when pancake and Goblins disagree there, pancake treats it as
pancake's bug. Two pancake peers can negotiate up to `pancake-next`, usually
with Cap'n Proto. Application code cannot tell the difference.

## Where pancake follows Goblins past the draft

If you already know OCapN, the interesting question is where pancake and the
draft specifications disagree. The answer is consistent: where the draft and
a running Goblins peer differ, pancake follows Goblins, because the wire has
to interoperate with what actually ships. It shows up in three places.

**Set and Keyword.** The pinned draft Model lists no set container and no keyword
atom. Goblins marshals both over [Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup), so
pancake carries them too: canonical set order with duplicate refusal, and the
Goblins `<kw …>` keyword record. Following the narrower draft list here would
mean failing to decode values a Goblins peer sends.

**No Null.** OCapN has no settled Null marshalling, and Goblins ships none, so
pancake mints no Null arm. An unknown Syrup record projects as a `Tagged` value
rather than a guess the partner could not read back.

**One signing domain.** Even when two peers run the Cap'n Proto codec,
pancake still signs over canonical Syrup bytes, the one form both sides can
reproduce. A session location signature and every handoff certificate are
Ed25519 over that form. The [Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) page has the detail.

## Who owns the wire bytes

Inbound bytes get borrowed before they get copied. The engine reads each
arriving frame through a **`FrameView`**, a borrow that lives for one
dispatch, and takes one owned **`Frame`** copy only when the event model has
to keep the message. Session tables never hold a borrow across a turn.

Two build tests pin the arithmetic: a steady inbound delivery costs exactly
one allocation, and a warmed session's core state sits near two kilobytes.
Read those as a design budget aimed at half a million sessions on one node.
Nobody has measured half a million live sockets yet, and
[Performance]($link.page('performance')) is careful about which is which; the
[architecture](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/architecture.md)
and
[ladder](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/ladder.md)
docs carry the budgets themselves.

## Distributed GC

Export table slots carry reference counts, and answer slots hold a single
reference that is released outright. With GC enabled on a binding, dropping a
fetched far reference makes the engine tell the peer, with `op:gc-exports` or
`op:gc-answers`, so unused slots leave the tables. It is off by default and
covers `fetch` results only; everything else keeps its row until the session
tears down. The
[GC](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/05-gc.md)
diagrams draw it.

## What proves it

Every semantic claim here is backed by something that runs:

- **Live Goblins interop.** `zig build goblins-interop` holds a real two-way
  OCapN conversation with a Spritely Goblins peer. The per-binding
  `*-goblins-interop` steps prove the client direction: keyed handshake,
  bootstrap fetch, and a call.
- **The upstream [OCapN test suite](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn-test-suite)**,
  run against a live pancake peer.
- **Cross-codec replay (pancake-to-pancake only).** The same conversation through
  Syrup and Cap'n Proto must produce an identical trace. That is not a Goblins
  parity claim.

[shipped](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/shipped.md)
inventories what those cover today, and [examples]($link.page('examples')) lets
you watch a round-trip. Ready to write the calling half yourself?
[Start building]($link.page('start')) walks it line by line.

[A friendly goblin sizing up a stack of pancakes. pancake is checked against a real Goblins peer, so the two have to agree on the wire.]($image.siteAsset('pancake-goblin-512.png'))

